{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.MarkovTraceTLUniqueness","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.MarkovTraceTLUniqueness","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.threeRow_ne_zero_of_ocneanu_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.threeRow_ne_zero_of_ocneanu_ne","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/MarkovTraceTLUniqueness.lean#L162-L179","doc":"**Convention C does not factor through Temperley–Lieb.**\n\nContrapositive of the uniqueness theorem, in the form that separates the two\nproduction conventions: a faithful normalised Markov trace whose Ocneanu\nparameter fails `z·[2]_q = q²` necessarily has a **nonzero** three-row weight.\nApplied to the Wenzl `z`-trace (convention C, `z = 1/(q^{1/2}+q^{-1/2})`), this\nsays C carries the `(1³)` block and so is not the Jones/TL trace. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markovWeights_degenerate_root","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markovWeights_degenerate_root","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/MarkovTraceTLUniqueness.lean#L146-L160","doc":"**The faithfulness hypothesis is not decorative.**\n\nThe Temperley–Lieb condition `y₁₃ = 0` alone has a *second* solution: the\ntrivial character `y = (1, 0, 0)` at `z = q`. It satisfies all three Markov\nequations, so `markovWeights_eq_jones_of_threeRow_eq_zero` genuinely needs\n`y₂₁ ≠ 0` — without it the conclusion `z·[2]_q = q²` is false (here\n`z·[2]_q = q² + 1 ≠ q²`). This solution annihilates the two-row block too, so\nit is not a faithful trace on `TL₃`; that is what rules it out. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markovWeights_eq_jones_of_threeRow_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.markovWeights_eq_jones_of_threeRow_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidsAndKnots/MarkovTraceTLUniqueness.lean#L104-L143","doc":"**The Temperley–Lieb condition pins the `H₃(q)` Markov trace uniquely.**\n\nLet `y₃, y₂₁, y₁₃` be the Frobenius weights of a normalised Markov trace on\n`H₃(q)` with Ocneanu parameter `z`, i.e. `tr = Σ_λ y_λ χ^λ` satisfying\n`tr 1 = 1`, `tr σ₀ = z`, `tr σ₀σ₁ = z²` against the `H₃(q)` character table\n(the three hypotheses `hnorm`, `hsigma`, `hsigmasigma`).\n\nIf the trace **factors through Temperley–Lieb** (`hTL : y₁₃ = 0`, the three-row\nblock is annihilated) and is **faithful on `TL₃`** (`hfaith : y₂₁ ≠ 0`), then\nthe weight vector and the Ocneanu parameter are completely determined:\n\n  `[2]_q² · y₂₁ = 1`,   `y₃ = 1 − 2 y₂₁`,   `z · [2]_q = q²`,\n\nwith `[2]_q = q + qi`. Solving the first for `y₂₁` recovers the Jones-closure\nvector `([4]_q/[2]_q³, 1/[2]_q², 0)` — convention D. ","signatureChunk":0}]}